    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1835</span><span class="rf-text">General Cos takes command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and the Siege of Béxar begins; Cos surrenders in December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1836</span><span class="rf-text">The Mexican army takes the Alamo after a 90-minute assault</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1836</span><span class="rf-text">Houston retreats eastward with his army and civilians in the Runaway Scrape</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1836</span><span class="rf-text">Texian scouts capture a Mexican courier carrying the locations and plans of the Mexican troops</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1836</span><span class="rf-text">The two armies camp about 500 yards apart near Lynch's Ferry and skirmish</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1836</span><span class="rf-text">The Texians storm the Mexican camp and win within 18 minutes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1836</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a> is captured; Cos is taken on 24 April</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1836</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a> agrees to the Treaties of Velasco and the Mexican army withdraws south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-san-jacinto" data-v="4967520a53" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies face each other across the grass</b> Houston drew up the Texian line with the second regiment on the left, Burleson in the centre, the artillery and Millard's companies on the right and the cavalry on the extreme right. The Mexican troops lay behind breastworks in their camp.</li><li><b>The cavalry go wide and the guns move up</b> The Texian cavalry was sent to the Mexican far left, and the artillery advanced through the tall grass to within 200 yards of the breastworks.</li><li><b>The Twin Sisters fire and the Texians rush the camp</b> The Twin Sisters fired and began the battle. After a single volley the Texians broke ranks and swarmed over the breastworks, and the surprised Mexican officers shouted conflicting orders.</li><li><b>The breastworks fall and the Mexican troops flee</b> The Texian infantry advanced until they held the woodland and the breastworks. Within 18 minutes the Mexican soldiers left their camp and fled. Santa Anna escaped towards Vince's Bridge.</li><li><b>Pursuit to Peggy Lake</b> Many Mexican soldiers retreated through the marsh to Peggy Lake, where Texian riflemen stationed themselves on the banks and shot at anything that moved. The killing lasted for hours.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna.jpg" alt="Antonio López de Santa Anna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Lopez_de_Santa_Anna_c1853.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meade Brothers, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> was a proponent of federalism when he helped oust Mexican President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-bustamante">Anastasio Bustamante</a> in December 1832. After his election as president in April 1833 he switched to centralist policies that increased the authority of his office. His abrogation of the Constitution of 1824 and abolition of local authority over the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a> y Tejas became a flashpoint between the central government and its Tejano and Anglo citizens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-f-austin">Stephen F. Austin</a>, who had urged a break-away state, was kept imprisoned for most of 1834.</p><p>In 1835 Santa Anna installed General Martín Perfecto de Cos as military governor of Texas. Cos set up headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> on 9 October, and the Siege of Béxar followed. Cos raised a white flag on 9 December and signed surrender terms two days later, which removed the occupying Mexican army from Texas. A circular of 30 December, known as the Tornel Decree, declared that foreigners who entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to join the rebellion were to be treated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and put to death if captured.</p><p>The Mexican Army of Operations numbered 6,019 soldiers. Santa Anna crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> on 16 February 1836 and took Béxar on 23 February. The Convention of 1836 signed the Texas Declaration of Independence on 2 March, and on 6 March the Mexican troops took the Alamo. General José de Urrea secured the Gulf Coast, and Houston learned of the surrender of James Fannin's men on 20 March.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar.jpg" alt="Mirabeau B. Lamar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar">Mirabeau B. Lamar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirabeaulamar_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author; derivative work by Oldag07, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Texian Army was commanded by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a>, whose military authority had been widened on 4 March to cover all Texian land forces. After the fall of the Alamo, volunteers swelled his ranks to about 1,400 men by 19 March, but many deserted during the retreat. Houston feared that his ill-trained and ill-disciplined force was good for only one battle. At Groce's Landing on the Brazos River the army drilled for the first time, and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> known as the Twin Sisters arrived from Cincinnati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>. Houston's army at the battle numbered around 800 men; the official count entering battle was 783.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a>, the president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, led about 700 men to Lynch's Ferry. On the morning of 21 April Cos arrived with 540 reinforcements, bringing the Mexican force to approximately 1,200 to 1,500. Cos's men were mostly raw recruits and had marched for more than 24 hours without rest or food. A further 4,000 Mexican troops remained under General Urrea and General Vicente Filisola.</p><p>Houston's army reached the ferry on 20 April and camped in woods along Buffalo Bayou, which hid its full strength but left no room to retreat. Over the protests of several officers, Santa Anna camped on a plain near the San Jacinto River, bordered by woods on one side and marsh and lake on another, about 500 yards from the Texians. Colonel Pedro Delgado later wrote that the site was against military rules. Houston placed Colonel Edward Burleson in the centre, the second regiment under Colonel Sherman on the left, the artillery under Colonel George W. Hackley on the right with four companies under Lieutenant Colonel Henry Millard, and 61 cavalry under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar">Mirabeau B. Lamar</a> on the extreme right.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Two brief skirmishes took place on 20 April. The Texians used the Twin Sisters to force a small group of dragoons and the Mexican artillery to withdraw, and Mexican dragoons then forced the Texian cavalry back. Thomas Rusk was almost captured on foot and was rescued by Lamar. Many Texian infantrymen rushed onto the field against Houston's orders, which angered him because it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a> a better estimate of their strength. During the night the Mexican troops built breastworks out of saddles, brush and whatever else they could find.</p><p>On 21 April, as the morning wore on with no Texian attack, the Mexican officers lowered their guard, and by afternoon Santa Anna had allowed Cos's men to sleep while the rest rested, ate and bathed. Houston had ordered Deaf Smith to destroy Vince's Bridge, about 8 miles from the camp, to block the only road out. The Texian cavalry went to the Mexican left, and the artillery advanced through tall grass to within 200 yards of the breastworks.</p><p>The Twin Sisters fired at 4:30 and the battle began. After a single volley the Texians broke ranks and swarmed over the breastworks, shouting "Remember the Alamo! Remember La Bahia (Goliad)!", and fought hand to hand. The Mexican soldiers were taken by surprise. Santa Anna, Castrillón and Almonte shouted conflicting orders. Within 18 minutes the Mexican soldiers had abandoned their camp and fled. Many retreated through the marsh to Peggy Lake, where Texian riflemen shot at anything that moved. Houston, Rusk and other officers tried to stop the killing but could not control men angry over the Alamo and Goliad. The killing lasted for hours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historian Davis called the victory one of the most one-sided in history: 650 Mexican soldiers were killed, 208 wounded and 300 captured, while 11 Texians were killed or mortally wounded and 30 wounded, Houston among them with a shot to the ankle. Texian soldiers gathered round the captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a>, who had fled towards Vince's Bridge, found it destroyed and hidden in the marsh, and called for his execution. He was captured on 22 April wearing a private's jacket, and other prisoners cried out when they recognised him. Cos was captured on 24 April.</p><p>Santa Anna wrote to Filisola, now the senior Mexican officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, that an unfortunate encounter had taken place, and ordered a retreat to Béxar. Urrea urged Filisola to continue the campaign, but Filisola did not wish to risk another disaster. Spring rains had ruined the ammunition and the roads, supplies had broken down and the troops were falling ill with dysentery. The Treaties of Velasco required all Mexican troops to withdraw south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> and Santa Anna to be given passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. In a secret version he promised to persuade the Mexican Congress to recognise Texas and the Rio Grande border.</p><p>Many former slaves followed Urrea's army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where they could be free, though the treaty required their return. Mexico refused to recognise any agreement signed by a prisoner, and Filisola was replaced by Urrea. The Mexican dead lay unburied on the battlefield for years. Rusk ordered the Tejanos between the Guadalupe and Nueces rivers to move east or to Mexico, and hundreds of Tejano families later resettled in Mexico.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Houston wrote a detailed first-hand account from the headquarters of the Texian Army at San Jacinto on 25 April 1836, and it gives the order in which he arrayed his troops. Colonel Pedro Delgado of the Mexican army later wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a>'s choice of camp, and Filisola wrote afterwards of his troops' plight on the retreat.</p><p>Strengths are approximate. The Texian force is put at around 800, with an official count of 783, and the Mexican force at approximately 1,200 to 1,500, a range that includes Cos's 540 reinforcements. The casualty figures are those given by the historian Davis. The mass grave of the Mexican dead has never been located.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pastry-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pastry-war.jpg" alt="Pastry War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pastry-war">Pastry War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%89pisode_de_l%27exp%C3%A9dition_du_Mexique_en_1838_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Houston became a national celebrity, and the cries "Remember the Alamo" and "Remember Goliad" became part of Texian history and legend. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a>'s treaties did not necessarily recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> as a sovereign nation but stipulated that he was to lobby for recognition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. In the months after the battle so many American volunteers flocked to the Texian army that the government could not keep an accurate list. Voters made Houston the first president of the Republic of Texas, ratified its constitution and approved a request for annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, authorities used the plan to reconquer Texas to justify new taxes and to put the army first in the budget, but larger expeditions were postponed as funds went to other rebellions. The northern Mexican states briefly launched an independent Republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> in 1839, and in June 1843 leaders of the two nations declared an armistice. Santa Anna was disgraced until the following year, when he became a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastry-war">Pastry War</a>.</p><p>The San Jacinto Battleground State Historic Site was designated a National Historic Landmark in 1960. It holds the 570 ft San Jacinto Monument, authorised on 21 April 1936 and dedicated on 21 April 1939, and holds a San Jacinto Day festival and battle re-enactment each April. The Texas Navy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> named ships after the battle, and the battleship USS Texas was anchored near the monument as a museum ship in 1948.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At San Jacinto on 21 April 1836 a Texian army of around 800 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a> overran the camp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a>'s Mexican force of approximately 1,200 to 1,500 in 18 minutes. Santa Anna was captured and agreed to withdraw his army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> refused to recognise Texan independence.</em></p>

